/**
@page logging Logging

Tesseract uses spdlog directly for logger ownership, severity filtering, formatting, sinks, and registry access. The
header `tesseract/common/logging.h` adds structured records and fan-out handlers for integrations that need more than
formatted text.

@section logging_native Native spdlog configuration

`tesseract::common::getLogger()` returns the registered Tesseract logger. Configure it with normal spdlog APIs:

@code
#include <tesseract/common/logging.h>

auto logger = tesseract::common::getLogger();
logger->set_level(spdlog::level::debug);
logger->set_pattern("[%Y-%m-%d %T.%e] [%n] [%l] %v");

TESSERACT_LOG_INFO("Loaded {} links", link_count);
TESSERACT_LOG_DEBUG_NAMED("tesseract.environment", "Revision {}", revision);
@endcode

Named loggers clone the default logger's sinks, formatter, level, error handler, and flush level when they are first
requested. Configure the default logger before creating named loggers when they should share the same initial setup.
Tesseract libraries should use the `TESSERACT_LOG_*` macros rather than call a logger directly so registered structured
record handlers also receive the event. Use stable subsystem names such as `tesseract.environment` or
`tesseract.collision.bullet`; do not create logger names from object identifiers or other transient values.

@section logging_records Structured records

The `TESSERACT_LOG_*` macros emit both the formatted spdlog message and a `tesseract::common::LogRecord`. A record
contains the original timestamp and severity, logger and optional component names, source location, thread ID, message,
and typed attributes. Use the macros for ordinary messages. Construct a record directly when an integration needs
machine-readable context that should not be encoded into the message:

@code
tesseract::common::LogRecord record;
record.level = spdlog::level::warn;
record.logger_name = "tesseract.environment";
record.component_name = "environment";
record.message = "Command rejected";
record.source_location = TESSERACT_LOG_SOURCE_LOCATION;
record.attributes.emplace("revision", std::int64_t{ revision });
record.attributes.emplace("command_type", command_type);
tesseract::common::emitLogRecord(std::move(record));
@endcode

Keep subsystem-specific values such as object identifiers, resource paths, operation names, and middleware endpoint
identifiers in `attributes`. Add a first-class `LogRecord` field only when it has stable semantics across Tesseract
subsystems and logging integrations.

Register a handler at the application boundary only when an integration needs structured records:

@code
auto id = tesseract::common::addLogRecordHandler(
    [](const tesseract::common::LogRecord& record) {
      exportRecord(record);
    });

// Remove the handler before destroying state captured by its callback.
tesseract::common::removeLogRecordHandler(id);
@endcode

Calls made directly through `spdlog::logger`, such as `logger->info(...)`, do not create a structured record and do not
invoke `LogRecordHandler` callbacks. Use the Tesseract macros or call `emitLogRecord()` when structured fan-out is
required. Handlers are additional outputs; they do not replace or reconfigure the logger's spdlog sinks.
Handlers run synchronously on the logging thread. A handler that performs network, disk, or other potentially blocking
work should enqueue a copy of the record and return promptly. Tesseract libraries must not install application logging
handlers or configure process-wide sinks and providers.

@section logging_ros2 ROS 2 adapter

The `tesseract_rosutils` package provides an adapter that forwards structured records to rcutils. Keep the RAII handler
alive for as long as forwarding is required:

@code
#include <tesseract_rosutils/log_record_handler.h>

tesseract_rosutils::RosLogRecordHandler ros_logging;
ros_logging.start();
@endcode

The severity mapping is trace/debug to DEBUG, info to INFO, warn to WARN, error to ERROR, and critical to FATAL. Logger
name and source location are passed natively to rcutils. The component name and typed attributes are appended to the
message as deterministic `key=value` text because rcutils has no structured-attribute API. rcutils assigns the ROS log
timestamp when it receives the record; its public logging API cannot accept the original Tesseract timestamp.
*/